#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128903 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs"}] :question/id #uuid "001529aa-c97a-5d69-82fb-cd4319a4dd7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3585" :question/problem "For the function $y=f(x)$ below, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?\n\n![](images/1a/1aeebec4df41080efc0d91a8adf00a799828e793e731ed7a36d32d881110596b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}} f(x) = -\\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^{-}} f(x) = \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=1.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is a point discontinuity, not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128904 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976f6e20-2c45-5123-bb60-738e099facc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "58f2bf5b-04b1-5931-8452-c095b55722ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-3586" :question/problem "Find all the points where the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-5x+6}$ has infinite discontinuities." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-3)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-2) = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=2,3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=2$ and $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea5bdd3b-810c-56e6-acfd-4de0e0cfc4e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions With Common Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "26d318a3-0b3f-50be-9802-92b10b83d119" :question/math-academy-id "e-3587" :question/problem "Find all the points where the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-4}$ has infinite discontinuities." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+3)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+2 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4317f5b8-cc60-5695-b5bb-47744cecc4cf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/id #uuid "83f4e413-e2f4-53dd-9893-fdf8e2df2642" :question/math-academy-id "e-3588" :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n![](images/2b/2b1f430a78861fce15c689843d0e4af5376e0f600b650b0a614257d86365cd29.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}$ or $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}$ grow without bound within their respective subdomains.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-5=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x < 5$, we do have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5^{-}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x-5}\\right) = -\\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=4.\n$$\n\nHowever, this branch of the function is defined for $x \\geq 5,$ which does not contain the neighborhood of $x=4.$ So $x=4$ cannot be a vertical asymptote, and therefore $x=4$ cannot be an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ only has a single infinite discontinuity, and it is at $x=5.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1e68cf3a87e981a07fdf6c8a2816ed25b26670b8afa274d95f05e7ea90ee340.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cbd5136-a5f7-5153-b44f-1bc8d1ae3939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504170} {:answer/id #uuid "5f92d4b5-2bf2-5695-8d9b-fe8032da20de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504198} {:answer/id #uuid "88a43f55-d606-5045-aa33-a88d589cabb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504269} {:answer/id #uuid "d604ce2c-70e7-5776-be54-5fe734224eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504405} {:answer/id #uuid "f409b383-6c6a-5fb1-a557-209e5d8e95c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f92d4b5-2bf2-5695-8d9b-fe8032da20de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504198} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf06f24-d4c1-55be-bb91-b1b50b76b77f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c798fdda-953e-5d91-a2f9-32a46093ebbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-104708" :question/problem "![](images/66/661520d112c7c7ef1c4591c3708f28fdb900d9ae6d036f774593239318205e09.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" :question/worked-solution "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 2.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-