is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "660212cb-c2c3-5b21-af6c-8a379432dede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504208} {:answer/id #uuid "81a75cde-a679-509f-9195-cf178aeeb372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504258} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb4f77a-dd07-5c58-93c9-049f9d325d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504283} {:answer/id #uuid "b9179029-3ffc-5187-a746-3312e1093839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504352} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a0eda9-4472-53ff-8909-b4853e9b27b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "660212cb-c2c3-5b21-af6c-8a379432dede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504208} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfe9a209-2cb9-5126-b3aa-2aa8747d8994" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f32b0e13-9234-5f0f-8ec6-055e7e55ffd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-104712" :question/problem "![](images/42/42266841cb4c8099a7ed7a0a5f5e14bc6f517c2a83bbeee7a02427d2ba44a208.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ \n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=1$ and $x=3,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976f6e20-2c45-5123-bb60-738e099facc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "054fd615-fb40-56d5-a49f-a713a52995ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504040} {:answer/id #uuid "2a02085f-873a-59de-9f9b-1c36d3550875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504107} {:answer/id #uuid "77d7a421-a902-5096-bf06-f339d78193d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504235} {:answer/id #uuid "d27fdc38-8f8f-5091-a646-100f38c0d44a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504390} {:answer/id #uuid "ede999ac-c321-5a94-ad9b-3718fd482490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a02085f-873a-59de-9f9b-1c36d3550875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504107} :answer-field/id #uuid "f32805e9-8f0e-5643-b8cd-67061494f218" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c899081f-aaf3-5d73-a009-6354d1a2e255" :question/math-academy-id "q-104715" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x}$ has infinite discontinuities." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x(x-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(x-5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=0$ and $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976f6e20-2c45-5123-bb60-738e099facc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "291228a4-c3a7-5174-9891-6983a1d3e47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504105} {:answer/id #uuid "46aa3791-0173-5d27-a875-8712bd8f9d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504159} {:answer/id #uuid "7adb8b4a-fc29-54b5-b7ea-67a5aa193245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504244} {:answer/id #uuid "861ca943-9dc9-55ba-919b-224eeb52d005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504265} {:answer/id #uuid "cf579c21-9449-5e46-8e89-8e2aa855f749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf579c21-9449-5e46-8e89-8e2aa855f749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504386} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec321b2-7a77-5fba-85b6-67cb523f2571" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d79ab0ee-343a-572d-8ea7-20c9b4a3405e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104830" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{2}-2x}$ has infinite discontinuities." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{x(3x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(3x-2)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4317f5b8-cc60-5695-b5bb-47744cecc4cf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "977816b7-4fd7-58d3-ad05-84b0516388b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504300} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cc2627-ca8c-5785-afee-c7aa3a6b9e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504351} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b3a3ba-da7b-5bc7-8ddc-f3120d8a60b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504374} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4ccd6c-60a5-5dcd-90a6-00a10987ff2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504385} {:answer/id #uuid "e717cc3f-4262-502e-ad3b-b445d589e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e717cc3f-4262-502e-ad3b-b445d589e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504431} :answer-field/id #uuid "39e0d4fd-93b9-5bf3-8ffa-8c8d4ca83b97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57542381-13e5-59f8-9ea4-61779c51f267" :question/math-academy-id "q-104854" :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has an infinite discontinuity.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & x\\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{x-3})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4317f5b8-cc60-5695-b5bb-47744cecc4cf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c0cd33a-fca9-5c78-8f3b-efa5df68b680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504083} {:answer/id #uuid "25291edd-4784-5a6b-bca1-a12e8664f92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504098} {:answer/id #uuid "4afab431-d446-53d4-92fd-4a3980b1aff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504169} {:answer/id #uuid "59b19465-a919-5d91-b4bb-f487a9c859aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No infinite discontinuities" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504188} {:answer/id #uuid "f2286a1f-5879-59e5-8aba-c9d1177ea0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-8$ and $x=-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25291edd-4784-5a6b-bca1-a12e8664f92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504098} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a17e3a-0fbe-5957-845d-21ab2cabc3a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdd8bd57-2d2f-5ace-b80e-0473ac5a0a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-104860" :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{x+8}, & x\\ne -8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $-\\frac{1}{x+8}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{1}{x+8}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-8.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{-}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{+}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-8$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed2cae2a50cb15f86ead5831990366db2048c100d71dc9d1b681b890da38844f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4317f5b8-cc60-5695-b5bb-47744cecc4cf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35dd2c60-3ba2-5d59-807e-7fa2a1be078f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35dd2c60-3ba2-5d59-807e-7fa2a1be078f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504124} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd59274-06d9-5099-a7f2-cee098896848" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8713a5b-a771-5f4a-a60c-95ad22b4fac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8713a5b-a771-5f4a-a60c-95ad22b4fac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504466} :answer-field/id #uuid "70aae3e0-86ce-5d9e-ac8d-3f5991ef70fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bccf9705-1345-5b91-b48e-5b2d9522eb17" :question/math-academy-id "q-334837" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the $x$-values  where the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-x}, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8-x}, & x\\ge 5,\\,x\\ne 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n has an infinite discontinuity are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,$ $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{5-x})=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=\\infty \\,,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5bb33c6-066f-5ab4-a3fd-eecbfa9a0aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5bb33c6-066f-5ab4-a3fd-eecbfa9a0aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504402} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb18eeee-c519-5ea4-9cf3-8aeda17a4734" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f8dfca9-4caa-51a0-a26a-4299455440e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340192" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6d3cbdc4a0158ebc827c2f991b446f0b9464322f8596b97176af55aa39b4a72d.png)\n\nBased on the graph of $y=f(x)$  shown above, the function $f$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0$  but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976f6e20-2c45-5123-bb60-738e099facc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccaf585f-0fb4-5dbb-9ce6-e9155c520ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccaf585f-0fb4-5dbb-9ce6-e9155c520ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504380} :answer-field/id #uuid "993a86cf-74ef-557e-a0ff-01bb8327b4da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504547} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9e945ae-cdd9-5d3a-b433-98f7ecd6b068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e945ae-cdd9-5d3a-b433-98f7ecd6b068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504413} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6672c5-0ab5-5bd9-ad56-fe83ceb63b7e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f60286b-a701-5a91-9733-7afc68b9ba06" :question/math-academy-id "q-340208" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the $x$-values  where the function  $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{3{x}^{2}+5x}$ \nhas discontinuities caused by vertical asymptotes are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,$ $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptote, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{3{x}^{2}+5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x(3x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptote by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(3x+5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{5}{3},0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-values  where the function  $f(x)$ has discontinuities caused by vertical asymptotes are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea5bdd3b-810c-56e6-acfd-4de0e0cfc4e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions With Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4328c095-7dda-5be5-8c37-d37e04b70e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4328c095-7dda-5be5-8c37-d37e04b70e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504151} :answer-field/id #uuid "8841289b-5c43-5bd8-834d-29ceebb7f817" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adf0b53d-c3e9-5c25-81fa-45a5c95eb4da" :question/math-academy-id "q-340229" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+20}{{x}^{2}-16}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+20}{{x}^{2}-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-5)(x-4)}{(x-4)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-5)\\cancel{(x-4)}}{\\cancel{(x-4)}(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-5}{x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+4=0\\,⟹\\,x=-4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea5bdd3b-810c-56e6-acfd-4de0e0cfc4e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions With Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed3047e-cde1-5f5b-9213-d32a09a9276f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed3047e-cde1-5f5b-9213-d32a09a9276f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504061} :answer-field/id #uuid "7be7d339-a2a1-5b1b-a9f2-f5e8a02e30de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f55b2c6c-0d34-5876-a0ba-01591a852f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340236" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x+3}{{x}^{2}+3x}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x+3}{{x}^{2}+3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(2x+1)}{x(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+3)}(2x+1)}{x\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea5bdd3b-810c-56e6-acfd-4de0e0cfc4e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions With Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5159ed8-3053-5c4e-8d85-9c95996a9ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5159ed8-3053-5c4e-8d85-9c95996a9ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504401} :answer-field/id #uuid "169b9ecc-de88-5ef3-b804-9017441f7f9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdf00b6e-c9c6-53a7-abba-5979171f25e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-340248" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{5-x}{3{x}^{2}-10x-25}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5-x}{3{x}^{2}-10x-25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(x-5)}{(3x+5)(x-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\cancel{(x-5)}}{(3x+5)\\cancel{(x-5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{3x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n3x+5=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4317f5b8-cc60-5695-b5bb-47744cecc4cf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac13e6e-13ad-59cb-b226-1c8376af83ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ac13e6e-13ad-59cb-b226-1c8376af83ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504080} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fcf9895-9e04-5cf4-b7d2-beef16232060" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28d2bedc-f4b0-58dc-80a4-886e3d716859" :question/math-academy-id "q-340259" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{x+3}, & x\\ne -3 \\\\ 2, & x=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if  $-\\frac{3}{x+3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{3}{x+3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48610f07da2103a0e1416318c1c70da426d8c845f0870387836098287d83efb8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4317f5b8-cc60-5695-b5bb-47744cecc4cf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4944b0a6-4855-57d9-b5a2-68a7cc64bcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4944b0a6-4855-57d9-b5a2-68a7cc64bcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504165} :answer-field/id #uuid "0935f3a5-2efe-5911-b0a9-10c3fad934c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af7b8af8-e50c-5536-8725-7ed61d38a3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-340282" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-1}, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\frac{2}{x-1})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976f6e20-2c45-5123-bb60-738e099facc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a657555-84bd-55c1-9848-f324c65b7490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{1}{2},$ $x=2,$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504109} {:answer/id #uuid "49ab0a29-3de9-5cef-ac37-2d8a93fc3a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504166} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcab203-19ca-5b4b-a3bd-a649dcac95cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504307} {:answer/id #uuid "baf07184-04cb-593e-895d-6a53654e7745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504355} {:answer/id #uuid "d4847fa0-fbf8-5d78-baae-1162e5806daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{1}{2},$ $x=-2,$ and $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49ab0a29-3de9-5cef-ac37-2d8a93fc3a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504166} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8975b1d-a5d0-5edc-93b1-10d5e6b649c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2b2de31-2e08-55fe-906d-b2e8d713411d" :question/math-academy-id "q-38828" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{2{x}^{2}-3x-2}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{2{x}^{2}-3x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(2x+1)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(2x+1)(x-2)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "164e26e6-dc38-5778-855d-3fd7a6a6d189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504070} {:answer/id #uuid "5851cb3d-8be7-5473-a9fb-3d39ae7b4c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504187} {:answer/id #uuid "a1362b30-f3f7-52c8-8b6c-57ff939d5594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504318} {:answer/id #uuid "ae379ffc-5816-5754-8d4e-c7835afac453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504332} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ae5329-c6b8-5034-9576-4d859de792a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "164e26e6-dc38-5778-855d-3fd7a6a6d189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504070} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6bec9c-e43c-5c8c-a9dd-a2c9d29fe6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41d68181-b01b-50ec-a85b-6bd02941be55" :question/math-academy-id "q-38909" :question/problem "![](images/74/744bb33e33eb84d02f53403b9f6ccd6077f6f9a09b5781ffdc31f61c6b5b8343.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976f6e20-2c45-5123-bb60-738e099facc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b46ada9-1c7a-5f43-8971-8d180ff44d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504054} {:answer/id #uuid "140e96dd-2459-59c5-bcaf-4fd8eead4254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504066} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecd5c78-1e1d-5836-9ee3-844fb1660c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504086} {:answer/id #uuid "34ff683c-d029-54f5-adfc-023dc5d8362d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504122} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4ccdd1-460e-5e41-b519-09b6141451af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e4ccdd1-460e-5e41-b519-09b6141451af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504196} :answer-field/id #uuid "b61093a5-492e-512c-8ebb-70f24dbe1a53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51bf4893-7d23-5880-bdd4-7c570a118b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-48242" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-3x-4}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-3x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-4)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-4)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1,4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=-1$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea5bdd3b-810c-56e6-acfd-4de0e0cfc4e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions With Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c28aa6-a7c1-5d7d-9fc7-a54775494788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504036} {:answer/id #uuid "429d7d5c-ff67-5ed2-8f5d-39a65a814039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504147} {:answer/id #uuid "66528a65-0099-55a8-90bd-4f89121be5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504209} {:answer/id #uuid "c089a137-69ac-5073-97a1-7b709089b090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504360} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f9b7c1-9257-5271-9658-6a650ce61670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03c28aa6-a7c1-5d7d-9fc7-a54775494788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504036} :answer-field/id #uuid "c126ee6e-ab7a-5db8-97ce-2fdafd3ea6ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3713990-073a-5c59-933e-791d16a2c49e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48247" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+2}{{x}^{2}-2x-3}$ has infinite discontinuities." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+2}{{x}^{2}-2x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x+2)}{(x+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+1)}(x+2)}{\\cancel{(x+1)}(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea5bdd3b-810c-56e6-acfd-4de0e0cfc4e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions With Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2133c5ee-8c48-5d6e-8606-63df09844a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504091} {:answer/id #uuid "442df143-0198-52eb-90fc-dd34179c54c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504154} {:answer/id #uuid "78237093-5c09-54d7-a37a-65c3003a4c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504236} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b00f01-bedd-5df5-aa99-b3a15f424ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504343} {:answer/id #uuid "e360a0d7-ac96-5460-848d-afe2f40227ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ and $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78237093-5c09-54d7-a37a-65c3003a4c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504236} :answer-field/id #uuid "33b5b008-f578-5dc2-bfdf-073208740399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9115fbc-4fac-58df-8796-79802b979ca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73429" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+13x+15}{2{x}^{2}-7x-15}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+13x+15}{2{x}^{2}-7x-15} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)(2x+3)}{(x-5)(2x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)\\cancel{(2x+3)}}{(x-5)\\cancel{(2x+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+5}{x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-5=0\\,⟹\\,x=5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=5$ only."}]}
